Need to implement the new deliveryInstructions field from this ticket. It's plain text supplied by support, unrelated to delivery_notes, which is a different internal warehouse field.

The product request is to persist the instructions on an order and include them in the order detail response for the new partner integration. Existing orders won't have instructions. The partner can treat an absent value as no instructions. There is no requirement to populate historical orders before launch.

The rough patch in the ticket currently looks like this:

ALTER TABLE orders ADD COLUMN delivery_instructions TEXT NOT NULL;

function serializeOrder(order) {
  return {
    ...existingFields(order),
    deliveryInstructions: order.delivery_instructions
  };
}

That serializer is shared by both generations of the order route, so please check the callers instead of blindly applying the sketch. We deploy instances gradually, and the previous application build can remain live for about twenty minutes after the schema job finishes. A rollback during that window also needs to work. Build the actual migration and response change around those conditions. The support write endpoint already accepts optional order metadata, so use that existing path rather than creating a new endpoint. No changes to the old mobile client's response are requested.

Fix the missing carrier details issue. The order endpoint still returns successfully, but every so often the carrier section is absent and support has no clue why. This isn't a request to change the response format or add fields.

I found this code while following the enrichment call:

async function addCarrierDetails(order) {
  try {
    const carrier = await carrierDirectory.lookup(order.carrierId);
    return { ...order, carrier };
  } catch (error) {
    return order;
  }
}

The directory contract distinguishes an unknown carrier from a transport failure. An unknown carrier is expected for old imported orders and should leave the order usable. A transport failure means we didn't actually learn whether that carrier exists. Right now both paths disappear into the same catch block, and callers can't tell a normal historical case from an outage.

Please inspect the existing error types and make the handling intentional. Preserve the expected behavior for imported orders, but don't turn every unexpected programming or network error into apparently valid missing data. We already have a logger in this module; no new logging framework is needed. Keep the explanation near whichever exceptional case we deliberately tolerate so the next person understands the choice.
